Update a known rumble effect in place instead of erasing it first

When a game uploads an effect id it has uploaded before (SDL does this for
every strength change), UI_FF_UPLOAD erased the vibrator's copy with
EVIOCRMFF and uploaded a new one. The erase stops a playing effect, so each
change switched the vibrator off and on: on the Pyra the TWL6040 driver
clears VIBCTLR and sets it again, and rumble stutters while a game changes
strength.

EVIOCSFF with an existing id updates the effect in place (input_ff_upload),
and a memless device such as twl6040-vibra keeps a started effect running at
the new magnitude. Re-upload under the vibrator-side id pyrainput already
keeps in _effectMap. If an earlier upload failed, the map holds -1 and the
same call uploads a fresh effect. If the vibrator refuses the id it holds,
fall back to the old erase and fresh upload.
A change of effect type never gets here: the uinput device's own ff core
refuses it before pyrainput sees the request.

Against funkeymonkey-pyrainput 0.1.19 (applies with or without 0001-0003).
Apply with: patch -p1 < this-file

--- a/joystick.cpp
+++ b/joystick.cpp
@@ -110,12 +110,21 @@
 					upload.request_id = ev.value;
 					ioctl(_fd, UI_BEGIN_FF_UPLOAD, &upload);
 					int orig = upload.effect.id;
+					bool updated = false;
 					if (_effectMap.find(orig) != _effectMap.end()) {
-						// effect already known, removing 1st
-						deleteEffect(orig);
+						// effect already known: update it in place under the
+						// device's id, so a playing effect keeps playing
+						upload.effect.id = _effectMap[orig];
+						updated = ioctl(_rumble, EVIOCSFF, &upload.effect) >= 0;
+						if (!updated) {
+							// the vibrator lost it, removing 1st
+							deleteEffect(orig);
+						}
+					}
+					if (!updated) {
+						upload.effect.id = -1;
+						ioctl(_rumble, EVIOCSFF, &upload.effect);
 					}
-					upload.effect.id = -1;
-					ioctl(_rumble, EVIOCSFF, &upload.effect);
 					_effectMap[orig] = upload.effect.id;
 					upload.retval = 0;
 					ioctl(_fd, UI_END_FF_UPLOAD, &upload);
